  "aiOverview": {
    "content": "The 1988–89 Michigan Wolverines men's basketball team won the school's first and only NCAA championship, defeating Seton Hall 80–79 in overtime on April 3, 1989. Led by interim coach Steve Fisher after Bill Frieder's departure, the team was led by Consensus All-American Glen Rice, who set an NCAA tournament record with 184 points. Key 1989 Team Details Record: 30–7 overall (12–6 in the Big Ten). Final Ranking: Ranked 10th in the final AP Poll. Championship Run: Entered the NCAA Tournament as a #3 seed in the Southeast Region. The Final: Rumeal Robinson hit two free throws with three seconds left in overtime to defeat Seton Hall, 80–79. Coach: Steve Fisher took over just before the tournament, finishing with a 6–0 record. Star Players: Glen Rice (MVP), Rumeal Robinson, Loy Vaught, Sean Higgins, and Terry Mills. Tournament Highlights First Round: Defeated Xavier 92–87. Second Round: Defeated South Alabama 91–82. Sweet 16: Defeated North Carolina 92–87. Elite Eight: Defeated Virginia 102–65. Final Four: Defeated Illinois 83–81. National Title: Defeated Seton Hall 80–79 (OT). Key Records & Awards Glen Rice: Named NCAA Tournament Most Outstanding Player and set the all-time Big Ten scoring record. Team Stats: The team led the nation in field goal percentage (56.6%). Big Ten Records: Set conference records for single-season points (3393) and single-season assists (745). This video shows highlights of the 1989 NCAA Championship game between Michigan and Seton Hall:1mMarch MadnessYouTube• Feb 7, 2025 1m The team is often recognized for its incredible resilience in transitioning to a new coach immediately before the NCAA Tournament.",
